{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Now hunt for me some game.</b> What did Yitzḥak see to seek a hunted thing — did he not have in his flock a kid of the goats whose taste is like the taste of deer, so that he had to send his son to a place of bands of wild beasts? And what seems closest to me to say about this is that it follows what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ḥullin 84a) regarding ״אֲשֶׁר יָצוּד צֵיד חַיָּה אוֹ עוֹף״ (Leviticus 17:13) — the Torah teaches you proper conduct, that a person should not eat meat except on this occasion. And the reason for this is so that a person not become habituated to eating meat, as it is written (Deuteronomy 12:20–22) ״בְּכָל אַוַּת נַפְשְׁךָ תֹּאכַל בָּשָׂר, אַךְ כַאֲשֶׁר יֵאָכֵל אֶת הַצְּבִי וְאֶת הָאַיָּל כֵּן תֹּאכְלֶנּוּ״ — and by this it wants you to eat ordinary meat only occasionally, not as a fixed habit, like deer and gazelle, which are not found in the house, for they are wild beasts, and their principal dwelling is not with man but in deserts and forests. Therefore one eats of them only a little, for it is not every day that a miracle happens to be saved from bands of wild beasts at the time of the hunt. So presumably a person eats of them only intermittently. Thus you will not accustom yourself to eating ordinary meat, because it produces cruelty and bad traits in a person's body — for all the birds of prey eat meat, and so too the lion preys and eats. Therefore it is said for the future (Isaiah 11:7) ״וְאַרְיֵה כַּבָּקָר יֹאכַל תֶּבֶן״, for there will be peace in the world among all living creatures. Therefore Yitzḥak said ״וְצוּדָה לִי צָיִדה״, for he did not wish to eat meat except on this occasion." "And Rashi explained: from that which is ownerless, and not from theft. And it appears to me to give a reason why he warned him about theft on this occasion more than at all other times — for 'hunt' has been in Yitzḥak's mouth these many years, and if he had already warned him about this, behold he stands warned, so why did he need to warn him a second time? And it appears that it is because by means of these tasty dishes Yitzḥak intended that the spirit of prophecy would come upon him from joy, and he thought that if he were to bring from theft, the Shekhinah would not rest in a place where it is found. For it was for this reason that Scripture warned about theft most of all at the beginning of the offerings, where it is written (Leviticus 1:2) ״אָדָם כִּי יַקְרִיב מִכֶּם״ — just as the first man did not bring from theft, etc. — because the offering brings near the Shekhinah while theft drives it away. And so too according to the opinion of the Midrash (Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer 32), which says: these two kids of goats — one he made as a Pesach-offering, etc.; and concerning the Pesach-offering it is written ״מִשְׁכוּ וּקְחוּ לָכֶם״ — from what is yours, to exclude the stolen — thus it concludes in the Yalkut, parashat Bo; and so Ba'al ha-Turim explained. And because Esav agreed in his heart to hunt game to bring even from theft, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, brought it about that Rivka overheard and sent Ya'akov in his place, so that Yitzḥak would not stumble in an offering that would come from something stolen. And it is possible that Esav indeed brought something stolen, for therefore it says ״וְעֵשָׂו אָחִיו בָּא מִצֵּידוֹ״, ״וַיַּעַשׂ גַּם הוּא מַטְעַמִּים״ — it mentions that he came from his hunt, but it did not mention that he brought something of his hunt, presumably because he did not find any, therefore he brought from theft. And Esav said ״וְיֹאכַל מִצֵּיד בְּנוֹ״, but the truth was not so. Therefore ״וַיֶּחֱרַד יִצְחָק״ — the meaning is that Gehinnom entered with him. And why did Gehinnom enter with him specifically on this occasion? Rather, presumably it is because he brought from theft — and whoever does this falls into that. And therefore we do not find that Yitzḥak ate from what Esav brought, for he sensed that there was something wrong, after Gehinnom had entered with him." "And the wording of the blessings will prove it, for he blessed him ״וְעַל חַרְבְּךָ תִחְיֶה״ (Genesis 27:40) — for one who inherits land by his sword, there is no greater armed brigand than him. And further, that in the blessing of Ya'akov it says ״וְיִתֶּן לְךָ הָאֱלֹהִים״ (Genesis 27:28), while in the blessing of Esav the language of giving is not mentioned, as it says ״הִנֵּה מִשְׁמַנֵּי הָאָרֶץ יִהְיֶה מוֹשָׁבֶךָ״ (Genesis 27:39) — because he brought something stolen, which is not a gift of Elohim. Therefore he blessed him in matters of brigandage, to say that by his sword he would inherit land and not by means of a gift of Elohim. But Ya'akov, who brought from what HaShem had given him — in his blessing it says ״וְיִתֶּן לְךָ הָאֱלֹהִים״. And Yitzḥak sensed this from the fact that the Garden of Eden entered with him, by means of the garments of Esav the desired ones, which he had coveted from Nimrod, which had come into his hand from the first man, who had worn them in the Garden of Eden. And Yitzḥak smelled the scent of the first man, and he sensed from this that this one's offering resembled the offering of the first man — who was unique in the world and did not bring anything stolen — so too this offering is not stolen. And therefore he blessed him with the blessing of ״וְיִתֶּן לְךָ הָאֱלֹהִים״."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {1 "The feminine pronoun suffix in 'שמעה' in the phrase 'שמעה רבקה' refers to Rivka overhearing; translated accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:42.913384463Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}